🧭 PHASE 1: RxJS BASICS (⭐ Important)

Goal: Understand what Observables are and how to use them.

🔹 Topic: Observable & Observer

Tasks:

Create a simple Observable that emits numbers 1, 2, 3 and logs them.

Create a custom Observable that emits your name after 2 seconds.

Implement an Observer with next, error, and complete methods.

Use of(), from() to convert arrays, promises, and strings into Observables.

🔹 Topic: Subscription (⭐ Important)

Tasks:

Subscribe to an Observable and log emitted values.

Unsubscribe manually after certain emissions using take() or unsubscribe().

Create a component that starts an Observable on ngOnInit and unsubscribes on ngOnDestroy.

⚙️ PHASE 2: CREATION OPERATORS (⭐ Important)

Goal: Learn how to create Observables efficiently.

🔹 Topic: Creation Operators

Tasks:

Use interval() and timer() to emit values at specific intervals.

Create an Observable using fromEvent(document, 'click') to log click positions.

Convert HTTP request mock data to Observable using of() or fromFetch().

Use defer() to create lazy Observables.

🔄 PHASE 3: TRANSFORMATION OPERATORS (⭐ Important)

Goal: Learn how to transform emitted data.

🔹 Topic: map, pluck, mapTo

Tasks:

Use map() to double all emitted numbers.

Use pluck() to extract specific properties from emitted objects.

Use mapTo() to replace emitted values with a constant string.

🔹 Topic: mergeMap, switchMap, concatMap, exhaustMap (🔥 VERY Important)

Tasks:

Mock two HTTP calls — chain them using:

mergeMap (parallel)

concatMap (sequential)

switchMap (cancel previous)

exhaustMap (ignore new until complete)

Demonstrate difference between them using a button click that triggers HTTP request.

🧩 PHASE 4: FILTERING OPERATORS

Goal: Control which emissions pass through.

🔹 Topic: filter, take, takeUntil, takeWhile, first, last

Tasks:

Emit numbers 1–10, use filter() to get even ones.

Use take(3) to take first 3 values.

Create a “Stop” button that calls takeUntil() to stop the stream.

Use first() and last() to log first and last emitted values.

🔹 Topic: debounceTime, distinctUntilChanged, throttleTime (⭐ Important for search)

Tasks:

Build a search box in Angular that:

Uses debounceTime(500)

Uses distinctUntilChanged()

Logs value only after user stops typing.

Compare it with throttleTime() behavior.

🧠 PHASE 5: COMBINATION OPERATORS

Goal: Combine multiple Observables.

🔹 Topic: combineLatest, withLatestFrom, merge, concat, forkJoin, zip

Tasks:

Combine two streams (e.g., first name + last name) using combineLatest().

Use withLatestFrom() to combine a timer and input events.

Merge click + keypress events using merge().

Concatenate Observables using concat().

Use forkJoin() to make 3 mock HTTP calls and emit when all complete.

Use zip() to pair emitted values from 2 streams.

⚡ PHASE 6: ERROR HANDLING (⭐ Important)

Goal: Handle errors gracefully in Observables.

🔹 Topic: catchError, retry, retryWhen

Tasks:

Create an Observable that throws error, handle it using catchError().

Simulate HTTP error and retry it 3 times using retry(3).

Use retryWhen() with delay before retrying.

🧹 PHASE 7: UTILITY OPERATORS

Goal: Manage side effects and finalize tasks.

🔹 Topic: tap, finalize, delay, timeout

Tasks:

Use tap() to log emitted values before mapping.

Simulate delayed response using delay(2000).

Use timeout() to handle slow Observables.

Add finalize() to show cleanup logic after Observable completes.

🧭 PHASE 8: SUBJECTS (⭐ Very Important)

Goal: Understand Subject types and behavior.

🔹 Topic: Subject

Tasks:

Create a Subject, subscribe from two observers, emit values manually.

Use Subject as EventEmitter between two Angular components.

🔹 Topic: BehaviorSubject (🔥 Crucial for Angular state)

Tasks:

Create a BehaviorSubject with default value.

Subscribe in multiple places and log latest values.

Use in a service to share state between components.

🔹 Topic: ReplaySubject and AsyncSubject

Tasks:

Demonstrate how ReplaySubject replays old values for late subscribers.

Show AsyncSubject emits only the last value when completed.

🧱 PHASE 9: HIGHER-ORDER OBSERVABLES (⭐ Important for complex flows)

Tasks:

Create an Observable that emits Observables.

Use switchAll(), mergeAll(), concatAll() to flatten them.

Combine them with map() to understand mapping + flattening.

🧰 PHASE 10: SCHEDULERS & CONCURRENCY

Tasks:

Use observeOn and subscribeOn with asyncScheduler to simulate background execution.

Experiment with execution order using queueScheduler.

🔄 PHASE 11: RxJS IN ANGULAR (🔥 Super Important)

Goal: Connect RxJS concepts with Angular ecosystem.

🔹 Topic: HttpClient with RxJS

Tasks:

Make a GET API call and transform data using map().

Handle API errors using catchError().

Retry API on failure using retry().

🔹 Topic: Forms & Reactive Programming

Tasks:

Use valueChanges of a FormControl with debounceTime() and distinctUntilChanged().

Cancel previous search API using switchMap() when new input arrives.

🔹 Topic: Component Communication using Subject (⭐ Important)

Tasks:

Use Subject in a service to send messages between components.

Use BehaviorSubject for shared state management (like logged-in user).

🔹 Topic: Async Pipe (⭐ Important)

Tasks:

Display Observable data in the template using async pipe.

Compare manual subscription vs async pipe (and memory leak prevention).

🧠 PHASE 12: ADVANCED PROJECT TASKS (Final Mastery)

Create a search autocomplete using FormControl.valueChanges + RxJS operators.

Build a live timer with start/pause/reset using interval() and Subject.

Implement real-time data refresh (every 5s) using timer() + switchMap().

Create retry mechanism for failed HTTP calls with exponential delay.

Implement a state management service using BehaviorSubject.       